--TEST--
An anonymous function made in a method that does not use `$this` captures no object, so `bindTo`, `bind` and `call` accept any object or `null` and the call returns the same result — `rule:statements/an-anonymous-function-captures-this-only-where-it-uses-it`, `rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `adder` makes its function inside a method, but the body never reads
// `$this`. So the function has no `$this`, and binding it changes nothing.
class Counter {
    public int $n = 1;

    public function adder(int $base): callable(int): int {
        return fn (int $x): int => $base + $x;
    }
}

class Other {
    public int $n = 99;
}

Counter $one = new Counter();
callable(int): int $add = $one->adder(10);
echo $add(1), "\n";

// An object of an unrelated class and `null` are both accepted.
echo $add->bindTo(new Other())(2), "\n";
echo $add->bindTo(null)(3), "\n";
echo $add->bind(new Counter())(4), "\n";
echo $add->call(new Other(), 5), "\n";
echo $add->call(null, 6), "\n";
--EXPECT--
11
12
13
14
15
16
